    goals: "Improve motor function and quality of life, manage symptoms with minimal side effects, prevent falls, plan for disease progression",
    pharm: "1. First-line therapy for significant motor impairment: Levodopa/Carbidopa (Co-careldopa) 100/25mg TID, increase gradually to 2 tablets TID as needed. 2. If dyskinesias or motor fluctuations develop: add entacapone 200mg with each Levodopa dose, or consider dopamine agonist (Pramipexole 0.125mg TID titrate slowly, or Ropinirole). 3. Alternative first-line in younger patients (<60): MAO-B inhibitor (Rasagiline 1mg daily, Selegiline 5mg BID) or dopamine agonist. 4. For tremor: Anticholinergic (Trihexyphenidyl/Benzhexol 2mg TID — use cautiously in elderly). 5. Osteoporosis prevention: Calcium + Vit D (fall risk).",
    nonPharm: "Physiotherapy: gait and balance training. Occupational therapy: home modifications, assistive devices. Speech therapy: LSVT (Lee Silverman Voice Treatment). Regular exercise (tai chi, cycling). Fall prevention: home safety assessment, remove rugs, grab bars.",
    carePlan: "Start Levodopa/Carbidopa 100/25mg TID with meals (or 30 min before meals). Titrate to symptom control over 4-8 weeks. Refer to physiotherapy, occupational therapy, and speech therapy. Optimise blood pressure control (avoid medications causing orthostasis). Review in 4 weeks.",
    dtps: "1. Progressive neurodegenerative disease requiring dopaminergic replacement. 2. Fall risk due to postural instability requiring multidisciplinary rehabilitation.",
    monitoring: "UPDRS motor score at each visit. Frequency and severity of off periods and dyskinesias. Fall diary. Orthostatic BP checks. MMSE/MoCA annually for cognitive decline. Assess for non-motor symptoms: depression, sleep, constipation, pain.",
    counselling: "Levodopa is the most effective medication for Parkinsons — it replaces the dopamine your brain is missing. It will significantly improve your movement and quality of life. Some people develop involuntary movements (dyskinesias) after years of use — this can be managed. Continue exercising — it helps slow disease progression. Do not stop medications abruptly (neuroleptic malignant syndrome).",
    followUp: "Neurology clinic in 4 weeks for dose titration. Physiotherapy ongoing. Annual cognitive screening. Monitor for disease progression and medication complications. Driving assessment if safety concern.",
    pearls: "Levodopa remains the gold standard for Parkinsons — no evidence that early use accelerates disease progression or complications. The \"levodopa sparing\" approach (starting with dopamine agonists or MAO-B inhibitors) is only for younger patients to delay motor complications. Protein-rich meals can reduce Levodopa absorption — take doses 30-60 min before meals if on/off fluctuations develop. Do not stop Levodopa abruptly (withdrawal can cause neuroleptic malignant syndrome).",
    references: ["NICE Guideline: Parkinsons Disease", "MDS Evidence-Based Review: Parkinsons Treatment", "Kenya Clinical Guidelines: Parkinsons"],
