    pharm: "1. SSRI: Sertraline 50mg daily for 1 week, then increase to 100mg daily (good previous response). 2. Adjunct for sleep/appetite (atypical features): Mirtazapine 15-30mg at bedtime (sedating, increases appetite, good for weight loss — however patient has increased appetite already, so use cautiously). 3. If minimal response at 6 weeks at 100mg: increase to 150-200mg daily or switch to alternative class (Venlafaxine or Bupropion). 4. If no response at 8 weeks to two adequate trials: refer for psychiatric consultation (consider ECT if severe with suicidality).",
    nonPharm: "Psychotherapy: Cognitive Behavioural Therapy (CBT) or Interpersonal Therapy — weekly for 12-16 sessions. Behavioural activation: re-engage in pleasurable activities gradually. Exercise: 30 mins of brisk walking 5 days/week. Sleep hygiene if hypersomnia. Marital counselling referral.",
    carePlan: "Start Sertraline 50mg daily. Assess suicide risk at each visit. Schedule weekly follow-up for first month (medication check + brief CBT). Refer for CBT. Encourage adherence — antidepressants take 4-6 weeks for full effect. Plan for 12 months of treatment after remission to prevent relapse.",
    dtps: "1. Recurrent depressive episode requiring pharmacotherapy and psychotherapy. 2. Suicidal ideation requiring close monitoring and safety planning.",
    monitoring: "PHQ-9 weekly until symptoms improve. Suicide risk assessment at every visit (ideation, plan, intent, means). Monitor for SSRI side effects (GI upset, sexual dysfunction, initial anxiety increase). Monitor for mood switch (mania/hypomania — rule out bipolar disorder).",
    counselling: "Antidepressants are not \"happy pills\" — they work by correcting a chemical imbalance in the brain. They take 4-6 weeks to work fully. Side effects like nausea or headache usually improve in 1-2 weeks. Do not stop abruptly (discontinuation syndrome). Therapy combined with medication is more effective than either alone. Reach out if you feel worse or have suicidal thoughts.",
    followUp: "Psychiatry/GP follow-up in 2 weeks (phone or visit). Continue monthly until remission, then every 3 months. Once fully remitted for 12 months, consider slow taper (over 4-6 months).",
    pearls: "SSRIs are first-line for moderate-severe depression — all SSRIs are equally effective but differ in side effects. Sertraline: good balance of efficacy and tolerability, low drug interactions, safe in overdose. Fluoxetine: longest half-life, best for adherence, worst initial anxiety. Atypical features (hypersomnia, hyperphagia, leaden paralysis) may respond better to MAOIs or SSRIs/SNRIs. Always assess for bipolar disorder before starting antidepressants (risk of mood switch). ECT is safe and highly effective for severe, treatment-resistant, or psychotic depression.",
    references: ["APA Guidelines: Major Depressive Disorder", "Lancet: Depression Treatment", "Kenya Mental Health Policy Guidelines"],
